# Track 4 — E2E round 2: 20 vCPU total budget
#
# Rebalanced (2026-06) after sweep analysis: E2E throughput tracks token-issuer
# REPLICA count (the issuer is HTTP-concurrency-bound per process). At 2 issuers
# R2 capped ~240/s with cost stuck ~1.1 (verifiers idle) — same wall as R1.
# Bumped issuer 2 -> 4 (funded from idle validators + trimmed selector/redis)
# so the 15 verifiers become the bottleneck instead.
#
# | Service              | cpus x replicas | subtotal |
# |----------------------|-----------------|----------|
# | proof-queue Redis    | 0.4 x 1         | 0.4      |
# | snark+token Redis    | 0.1 x 2         | 0.2      |
# | request-handler      | 0.75 x 2        | 1.5      |
# | verifier-selector    | 0.3 x 1         | 0.3      |
# | token-issuer         | 0.5 x 4         | 2.0      |
# | snark-verifier       | 1.0 x 15        | 15.0     |
# | token-validator (x5) | 0.12 x 5        | 0.6      |
# | TOTAL                |                 | 20.0     |
#
# Expectation: throughput should climb from ~240 toward ~380-420 (the 15-verifier
# ceiling), cost rising past ~1.1. R2's 5-vCPU overhead is genuinely tight, so it
# may land handler-limited around ~400 rather than fully verifier-limited — still
# a big jump from 240, and a legitimate "small-budget overhead dominance" result.
#
# Load with:  set -a; source e2e-throughput-round2.env; set +a
PROOF_CPUS=0.4
REDIS_CPUS=0.1
HANDLER_REPLICAS=2
HANDLER_CPUS=0.75
SELECTOR_CPUS=0.3
ISSUER_REPLICAS=4
ISSUER_CPUS=0.5
SNARK_REPLICAS=15
SNARK_COUNT=15
# Applied manually on each App EC2: docker update --cpus=$VALIDATOR_CPUS ...
VALIDATOR_CPUS=0.12
